1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:02,660
Wealth, Fame, Power.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:04,597
Gold Roger, the King of the Pirates,

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,197
attained this and everything
else the world had to offer,

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,456
and his dying words drove
countless souls to the seas.

5
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:11,975
You want my treasure?

6
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,420
You can have it.

7
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:16,620
I left everything I gathered
together in one place.

8
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,060
Now you just have to find it.

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:24,217
These words lured men to
the Grand Line in pursuit

10
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,737
of dreams greater than
they'd ever dared to imagine.

11
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:29,517
This is the time known
as the Great Pirate Era.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,980
Come aboard and bring along
all your hopes and dreams.

13
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:40,440
Together we will find everything
that we're looking for.

14
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,700
One piece.

15
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,577
Compass left behind,
it'll only slow us down.

16
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:58,417
Your heart will be your guide,
raise the sails and take the helm.

17
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,137
That legendary place.

18
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:03,817
That the end of the map reveals.

19
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,677
Is only legendary.

20
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:10,117
Till someone proves it real.

21
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,557
Through it all.

22
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,497
Through all the trouble and times.

23
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:16,817
Through the heartache
and through the pain.

24
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:21,317
Know that I will be there to stand by you.

25
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,160
Just like I know you'll stand by me.

26
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:30,540
So come aboard and bring along
all your hopes and dreams.

27
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,020
Together we will find everything
that we're looking for.

28
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:42,420
There's always room for you
if you want to be my friend.

29
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:46,860
We are, we are on a cruise.

30
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:49,700
We are?

31
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:01,391
There's absolutely no way we'll make
it to the Grand Line in this condition.

32
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:07,174
You're right, we're going to
need a whole lot more meat.

33
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,597
Eating fruit every day is for the birds.

34
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:12,268
We'll also need some booze.

35
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:14,060
Shut up, you boneheads!

36
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:17,797
That's not what I mean.

37
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:19,617
The Grand Line's a dangerous place.

38
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,617
We'll need more than beer
and meat to survive there.

39
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,437
Not to mention the place is crawling with

40
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,357
pirates who are also in
search of the one piece.

41
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,917
And they all have ships that
are way better than this!

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,397
And we don't have a crew.

43
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,077
We don't have a crew, we
don't have any equipment,

44
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:33,657
or even a real ship for that matter.

45
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,477
There is no way we'll
ever make it like this.

46
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:39,897
Yep, we'll need a cook, and maybe
a musician to keep us entertained?

47
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,040
A musician?

48
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:45,010
So stupid?

49
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:49,636
A little to the south of here is an
island with some settlements on it.

50
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:51,957
And it looks like we
might be able to get our

51
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,757
ship and some of the
supplies we need there!

52
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,700
Fresh meat at last!

53
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:57,300
Lots of booze as well

54
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:03,840
Sound the alarm! They're here!

55
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,569
They're here and they're
gonna kill us all!

56
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,480
Oh no! Pirates! Pirates!

57
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:12,580
Pirates are attacking! Run!

58
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:17,737
Everybody run for your
lives! Pirates! Pirates!

59
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,360
Everybody run!

60
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:20,738
Pirates are coming!

61
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:24,140
Their bloodlust is unquenchable!

62
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:29,100
We're doomed.

63
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:31,820
The pirates are...

64
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:34,560
Lie!

65
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,420
That hurt!

66
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:42,820
You little liar!

67
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,178
You're not gonna get away this time!

68
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,340
Wait, wait, wait!

69
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:50,870
Now run!

70
00:03:57,860 --> 00:03:59,340
Just try and catch me!

71
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:00,920
Come on, slowpokes!

72
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:06,560
Ah, man!

73
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:08,646
Looks like he got away again!

74
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,160
That kid's a Newgate troublemaker.

75
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,757
He'd better hope I
never get a hold of him.

76
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,664
Looks like nobody's getting
a hold of him today.

77
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,800
Ah, yet another job well done.

78
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:32,897
It is my solemn duty to send the wind of
excitement through this boring old village.

79
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:34,160
There he is!

80
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:35,890
Hmm?

81
00:04:35,973 --> 00:04:37,320
Hey, you guys, what's going on?

82
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:39,754
I bid you, good morning, captain Usopp!

83
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:43,531
Fear not, your loyal crew, the
Usopp Pirates, have arrived!

84
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:46,820
Pepper! Carrot!

85
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:47,837
It's just you two?

86
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:48,750
Where's Onion?

87
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:50,304
Have you seen him around today?

88
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,960
Sound the alarm! They're
here! They're here!

89
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:00,040
They're here and they're
gonna kill us all!

90
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:01,560
Hey, it's Onion.

91
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:03,937
I wonder what he's freaking out about?

92
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:04,850
Pirates!

93
00:05:04,933 --> 00:05:05,763
Pirates!

94
00:05:05,846 --> 00:05:06,676
They're here!

95
00:05:06,759 --> 00:05:07,589
Run for your lives!

96
00:05:07,672 --> 00:05:08,502
Pirates!

97
00:05:08,585 --> 00:05:09,415
We don't stand a chance!

98
00:05:09,498 --> 00:05:10,328
Bloodthirsty murderers!

99
00:05:10,411 --> 00:05:11,445
If Lincoln run, everybody run!

100
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:13,540
Stop playing!

101
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:17,340
I'm not playing!

102
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:19,620
It's for real?

103
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:21,197
Yes, it's for real!

104
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,280
Pirates are here!

105
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:24,490
Gotta go!

106
00:05:24,573 --> 00:05:27,080
Time for me to have my snack! You chicken!

107
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,100
Actually, I'm sick! Yeah, I have!

108
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:33,455
I'll die if I don't eat my snackiosis!

109
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:35,660
You liar!

110
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:38,517
You always told us that
you wanted to become

111
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,677
a real pirate someday
and now's our chance!

112
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:42,280
Yeah, what's with you?

113
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:44,777
What kind of a pirate is
afraid of other pirates?

114
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,978
If I recall correctly, it
was Buggy the Clown's mark.

115
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:50,340
Buggy the Clown?

116
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:55,760
I think I'll just...

117
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,540
But I only saw two tiny boats.

118
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:00,981
And there were just three people.

119
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:05,640
It's go time, brave Usa pirates!

120
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:07,822
We do have a village to protect.

121
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:10,260
Let's go, men! Move out!

122
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:13,250
Sir!

123
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:24,780
Feels good to be on dry land again.

124
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:27,997
There's a village around
here somewhere, right?

125
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:28,910
Yeah.

126
00:06:28,993 --> 00:06:30,420
It looks like a small one, though.

127
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,070
Ooh! Ooh!

128
00:06:32,153 --> 00:06:33,099
Then there's meat!

129
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:34,030
There's meat!

130
00:06:34,113 --> 00:06:36,537
There has to be a place to
eat meat in this village!

131
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:39,326
Just forget about meat
for a minute, would ya?

132
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:42,850
Careful.

133
00:06:42,933 --> 00:06:45,677
We're being watched. Huh?
By who? Where are they?

134
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:46,590
They got meat?

135
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:47,630
Look out!

136
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,500
That's so cool!

137
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,328
Is this supposed to be
when we're impressed?

138
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:09,217
I am Usopp, leader of the invincible nation
of pirates who reign over this island.

139
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:13,297
You stand before a living legend, the
most revered pirate who ever lived.

140
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:15,160
I am the great Captain Usopp.

141
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:21,357
Surely you know that plotting an attack
on this island would mean certain death.

142
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,911
My army of 80 million super mean pirate
soldiers would squash you like bugs.

143
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:27,790
Awesome!

144
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,820
You expect us to believe that?

145
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:35,680
She knows.

146
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:37,630
Yep.

147
00:07:37,713 --> 00:07:38,543
Well, now I know.

148
00:07:38,626 --> 00:07:40,087
How can I have been so dumb?

149
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:41,817
I practically told her I was lying!

150
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:42,937
I'm such an idiot!

151
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:43,850
So stupid!

152
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:46,950
What?

153
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:47,863
He was lying?

154
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:49,982
Curse you, you crafty tactician!

155
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:52,377
80 million may have been an exaggeration,

156
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,107
but I have a great many
men under my control!

157
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,200
My guess is three.

158
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:06,720
She does know!

159
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:12,900
Hey, where are you guys going? Come back!

160
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:17,680
That's pretty cool!

161
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:21,732
Shut your mouth, or I'll shut it for you!

162
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,371
I'm a proud man, and I won't stand for it!

163
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:28,761
This is your last warning, or you'll
get to see why they call me...

164
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:32,160
Proud Usopp, and not pushover Usopp.

165
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:34,300
Is that clear?

166
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,357
You've already seen for yourself
just how good I am with this thing.

167
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:41,381
So, you know I can rival
anyone carrying a pistol.

168
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:51,800
Now that you've drawn your
pistol, are you willing to use it?

169
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:56,477
Guns aren't for threats,
they're for actions.

170
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:58,560
Are you willing to risk your life?

171
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:17,817
Real pirates definitely
have a lot more punch behind

172
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,237
their words because they
can actually back them up.

173
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:21,420
That's so cool.

174
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:29,300
I stole that. Huh?

175
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:33,209
I stole those words from
Red-Haired Shanks, a pirate I know.

176
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:34,590
What?

177
00:09:34,673 --> 00:09:35,877
You know Red-Haired Shanks?

178
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:37,237
You really are a real pirate.

179
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:38,737
Wait, you gotta be lying.

180
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:40,500
Do you really know him? Yep.

181
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:42,320
I know your dad, too.

182
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:44,540
His name's Yasop, right? Huh?

183
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:53,730
You're right.

184
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
My old man's name is Yasop.

185
00:09:56,800 --> 00:10:01,440
But how, uh, how in the
world do you know him?

186
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:12,319
This is good, huh?

187
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:17,085
So I met your dad when
I was just a little kid.

188
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:19,557
Hold on, does my old
man really know Shanks?

189
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:20,470
Know him.

190
00:10:20,553 --> 00:10:22,497
When I met your dad, he
was part of Shanks' crew,

191
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:24,517
so they were probably pretty good friends.

192
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:25,960
I can't believe it!

193
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:29,157
My old man's sailing the high
seas with famous pirates.

194
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:33,240
I haven't heard from him in a while,
but I bet he's still with them.

195
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:35,922
He was a heck of a sharpshooter.

196
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:37,660
I'd keep him on my crew.

197
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:55,960
Whoa! Good shot!

198
00:10:57,240 --> 00:10:58,257
Eh, that's nothing.

199
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:01,575
I could shoot an ant between
the eyes from across town.

200
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:07,180
You know something, Luffy?

201
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:11,137
I got a kid named Usopp, and
he's just about your age.

202
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:13,897
You told me about him
already, a hundred times.

203
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:14,810
A hundred?

204
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:17,880
Well, too bad.

205
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:19,700
I'm telling you again.

206
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:24,180
It broke my heart to
leave, but I had to go.

207
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:29,240
You see, Luffy, when the pirate flag
beckons, you must hate its call.

208
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:42,367
He used to go on and on
and on about his son.

209
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:44,857
I think I heard enough about
you to make my ears bleed.

210
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:47,297
And let me tell you,
he was a great pirate.

211
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:48,240
Yeah, really?

212
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:50,760
I sure am proud of my old man.

213
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:53,842
He leapt into a pirate's
life on the endless sea.

214
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:57,079
And chose a life of real adventure.

215
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,460
Maybe you can help us.

216
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:03,117
Do you know someone who could
provide us with a decent ship,

217
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:06,318
or even anyone who knows
how to sail for that matter?

218
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Well, it's a pretty small village.

219
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:12,337
Sorry, but I just don't think
we'll be able to help you.

220
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:14,126
What about that huge mansion?

221
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:16,774
The one on top of that hill over there.

222
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:18,900
Hey, do you know the Moosop?

223
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,297
With all that money, surely
they can help us get our boat.

224
00:12:22,380 --> 00:12:23,851
You stay away from there!

225
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:29,620
I just remembered something
that I really gotta do.

226
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:34,818
Everybody knows me at this place,
so stay and eat as much as you want.

227
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:36,740
Well, I gotta go!

228
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:42,326
Why was he in such a
hurry to get out of here?

229
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,717
Are you sure this is
the place they took him?

230
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:50,597
Yeah, those terrible
pirates dragged him inside,

231
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:52,317
and now it's up to us to save our captain.

232
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:53,717
But guys, those are real pirates.

233
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:55,377
What if they've grilled
and eaten him already?

234
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:58,637
It's grisly old witch hag to
eat their victims, not pirates!

235
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:01,857
This is going to be the most brutal
and epic battle of our lives.

236
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:05,352
We must show no mercy because
they'll never show it to us.

237
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:09,040
The Usopp pirates have arrived!

238
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:10,720
Who are these kids?

239
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:13,240
Captain Usopp is gone. Did they?

240
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:15,807
Vice up, you filthy pirates!

241
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:17,717
What have you done with
our beloved captain?

242
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:18,630
Tell us now!

243
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:19,860
Yeah, tell us now!

244
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:22,860
That meat sure was good.

245
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:24,430
What meat?

246
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,287
These pirates are cannibals!

247
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:30,000
What have you done, you savages?

248
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:32,920
If you're looking for your captain...

249
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:35,940
We ate him.

250
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:40,020
Ah, careful!

251
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,876
What are you punks looking at me for?

252
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:47,542
You just had to say
something stupid, didn't you?

253
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:00,440
You wish to see Usopp.

254
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:02,960
Don't start this again.

255
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:04,840
May I see him?

256
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:06,480
I like talking to him.

257
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:08,820
No, ma 'am.

258
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:11,689
That man is without a
doubt a chronic liar.

259
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:13,857
For someone in your weakened state,

260
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:17,097
Usopp's outlandish stories
might be too much to handle.

261
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:21,437
Your late parents instructed me to protect
you to the fullest of my abilities.

262
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:24,997
It would be against their wishes
for you to see him, Miss Kaya.

263
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:27,786
Now then, I'll leave
your medicine right here.

264
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:29,429
Please be sure to take it.

265
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:35,770
Killjoy.

266
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:37,100
Killjoy will do.

267
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:39,655
A name I'll bear if you are kept safe.

268
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:53,320
Usopp, you came! Of course. How are you?

269
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:54,657
You seem a little down.

270
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:55,860
I guess I am.

271
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:57,317
I'm tired of talking like this.

272
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:00,317
I'd like to invite you
inside like a proper guest.

273
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,635
But there's just no way
Klahadore would ever allow that.

274
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:06,740
He's not a bad person,
though. Don't sweat it.

275
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:10,877
Remember, I'm a brave warrior of the sea
who likes endless oceans and the open sky.

276
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:12,697
Houses weren't made for rogues like me.

277
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:15,133
I'd probably just feel cramped in there.

278
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,535
So what adventure are you
going to tell me about today?

279
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:19,850
Hmm.

280
00:15:19,933 --> 00:15:20,763
Well, let's see.

281
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:22,080
Oh, I know.

282
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:24,537
What about the story of me
fighting a giant goldfish

283
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:26,817
at the South Pole when I
was only five years old?

284
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:27,920
A goldfish?

285
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:29,500
A giant goldfish!

286
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:32,817
What first astounded me was
the immense size of its poop!

287
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:36,217
It was so big that when I landed on
it, I thought it was solid ground!

288
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:37,771
It looked like an island!

289
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:53,630
Wow!

290
00:15:53,713 --> 00:15:54,757
This place is huge!

291
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:58,957
And if our captain ran off at this hour,
then this is the place he's going to be.

292
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:01,805
Oh, what in the world
does Usopp come here for?

293
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:04,780
He comes to lie. To lie? That's terrible.

294
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:06,158
He's not a bad guy.

295
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:07,500
He's great, isn't he?

296
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:09,160
Yeah, he's awesome.

297
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:10,940
How is a liar awesome?

298
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:13,580
Well, Kaya,

299
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:17,177
the girl who lives there has been
in a terrible depression that's kept

300
00:16:17,260 --> 00:16:21,260
her weak and in bed since her
parents got sick and died a year ago.

301
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:26,460
She's very rich, but all that money
couldn't help her recover from her sadness.

302
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:30,897
So our captain tells us special made-up
stories to make her laugh and cheer her up.

303
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,440
Captain Isop is the best at telling lies.

304
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:39,617
Wow, that really is pretty nice of him.

305
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:40,530
Yeah!

306
00:16:40,613 --> 00:16:43,057
The best thing about him
is his mischievous side.

307
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:44,517
He's always up to something.

308
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:46,677
I like him because he's
not afraid to be silly.

309
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:48,777
And I like the captain's crazy lies.

310
00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:52,257
So when he tells her those
stories, he makes her feel better?

311
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:54,580
Yep, lots better. Great!

312
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:57,800
I think we should ask
her for a ship after all!

313
00:16:58,220 --> 00:16:59,691
Getting in is impossible.

314
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:02,697
Those guards at the gate
don't let anyone through.

315
00:17:02,780 --> 00:17:04,300
Oh, where's Luffy?

316
00:17:04,760 --> 00:17:05,920
I'll go ask her!

317
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:08,413
No, you're not gonna...

318
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:10,170
No!

319
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:14,640
Pardon the intrusion!

320
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:16,178
No, no, no, no, no!

321
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:23,777
So after that terrifying and
traumatic little incident,

322
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:27,440
we escaped into the sky riding
on a giant slingshot ball!

323
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:29,800
What's that?

324
00:17:31,140 --> 00:17:31,970
Yahoo!

325
00:17:39,480 --> 00:17:41,000
That worked well.

326
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:42,860
My goodness, are you okay?

327
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:45,260
Ah, don't mind them, Kaya.

328
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:46,577
This happens to me all the time.

329
00:17:46,660 --> 00:17:48,577
That's just the price of
being a famous adventurer.

330
00:17:48,660 --> 00:17:52,357
They're just new recruits who heard about
me and came to join the Usopp Pirates.

331
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:53,620
Nope, not me.

332
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:54,997
I came here to see you.

333
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:56,727
I wanted to ask you a favor.

334
00:17:56,900 --> 00:17:58,940
A favor from me? Uh-huh.

335
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:01,937
I was wondering if there was any
way you could help us get a boat.

336
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:02,850
You there.

337
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:05,964
What do you think you're doing?

338
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:09,310
Klahadore.

339
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:11,910
Get out.

340
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:16,658
I'll have you know that this
is criminal trespassing.

341
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,517
These people are...

342
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,560
Not now, Kaya.

343
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:27,231
You can tell me all about this
riffraff after they've left.

344
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,620
Now get out, all of you.

345
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,817
Leave now, unless you have
some business with me.

346
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:33,730
All right.

347
00:18:33,813 --> 00:18:36,051
Do you think you could help us get a ship?

348
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:37,240
Certainly not.

349
00:18:38,520 --> 00:18:39,920
Usopp! Oh, crap!

350
00:18:40,300 --> 00:18:41,737
You have quite the reputation.

351
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:44,540
You are very well known in the village.

352
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:47,997
Quite impressive for
someone as young as you!

353
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:50,737
Usually people who admire
me so much call me Captain.

354
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:53,120
So it's Captain Usopp to you.

355
00:18:54,180 --> 00:18:57,540
Your father also had something
of a reputation. What?

356
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:01,547
You'll never be anything more
than the son of a filthy pirate.

357
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:04,637
Who knows what sort of
trouble you're up to now?

358
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:09,520
All I ask is that you and your kind
leave this respectable young lady alone.

359
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:12,000
A filthy pirate?

360
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:15,957
You and she are from completely
different worlds, and you know it.

361
00:19:16,040 --> 00:19:17,317
Is it money you're after?

362
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:18,635
How much do you want?

363
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:21,417
Cease this at once, Klahadore!

364
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:22,500
Apologize to him!

365
00:19:23,240 --> 00:19:24,840
Hey, calm down, Kaya.

366
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:29,862
I don't believe there is any reason to
apologize to this savage young man, my lady.

367
00:19:30,480 --> 00:19:32,200
My goal isn't to slander.

368
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,197
I am merely stating the truth.

369
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:35,957
I sympathize with your situation.

370
00:19:36,040 --> 00:19:38,657
You must hate your
great idiot of a father?

371
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:40,657
For abandoning his family and his village.

372
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:43,211
because of his mindless lust for treasure.

373
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:44,560
Clador, stop!

374
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:48,100
Don't you dare insult
my father ever again!

375
00:19:50,060 --> 00:19:50,890
Usopp?

376
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:02,040
Why have you gotten so worked up, Usopp?

377
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:05,257
It's times like these when you
should do what you do best and lie.

378
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:08,797
Tell me about how your father
is really a traveling salesman,

379
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:11,704
or about how you aren't
really related by blood.

380
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:23,510
Captain!

381
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:26,960
Case in point.

382
00:20:27,240 --> 00:20:28,593
He resorts to violence.

383
00:20:28,760 --> 00:20:31,517
I guess the apple never falls
far from the tree, does it?

384
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:32,430
Shut up!

385
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:37,097
My father listened to his heart and
chose a life of risk and adventure.

386
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:40,557
I am proud to say that my old man
is a brave warrior of the sea.

387
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:43,417
You're right, I have been known
to exaggerate from time to time,

388
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,797
but I will never lie
about who my father is.

389
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:48,240
I refuse to be ashamed of my heritage.

390
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:50,820
I am the son of a pirate.

391
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:56,550
Captain.

392
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:02,024
Pirates are brave warriors
of the sea, are they?

393
00:21:03,700 --> 00:21:06,177
That's quite a twisted
way of putting things.

394
00:21:06,260 --> 00:21:10,157
You don't seem to understand that you
are living proof of his savage blood.

395
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:13,857
You run around spouting lies and use
violence when you don't get your way.

396
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:18,234
To top it off, you cozy up to the lady
of the house to steal her fortune.

397
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:20,477
You're wrong! I only...

398
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:22,837
Your father is a filthy, Newgate pirate!

399
00:21:22,920 --> 00:21:24,257
Treachery is in your blood!

400
00:21:24,340 --> 00:21:26,197
Of course you would have
such an evil scheme!

401
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:27,837
You won't let up, will you?

402
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:29,273
Stop it, Usopp, please!

403
00:21:30,460 --> 00:21:31,820
No more violets!

404
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:33,120
I can't take it!

405
00:21:34,420 --> 00:21:36,220
Lahador isn't a bad person.

406
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:42,220
He's just trying to do what he thinks is
best for me, and sometimes he goes too far.

407
00:21:45,780 --> 00:21:47,180
Leave this place.

408
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:49,800
You are never to come here again!

409
00:21:51,200 --> 00:21:52,700
Yeah, I get it.

410
00:21:53,040 --> 00:21:55,334
You don't have to tell me twice, buddy.

411
00:21:56,660 --> 00:21:58,954
This is the last you'll ever see of me.

412
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:02,370
Usopp.

413
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:05,740
Hey, you butler jerk!

414
00:22:05,980 --> 00:22:08,080
Our captain's not that kind of guy!

415
00:22:12,640 --> 00:22:15,464
Shut up, you don't have
a bone to pick with him.

416
00:22:18,220 --> 00:22:19,580
You're a bunch of dope!

417
00:22:19,860 --> 00:22:21,457
Pull me down! Pull me down!

418
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:22,893
I'm gonna kick his ass!

419
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:25,220
You will all leave immediately!

420
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,220
Your meal is ready now, Miss Kaya.

421
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:41,320
Thank you.

422
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:42,820
I don't want it.

423
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:49,841
The cook would be most
upset to hear you say that.

424
00:22:50,260 --> 00:22:52,477
He works his hardest day and night to come

425
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:55,207
up with delicious meals
that suit your needs.

426
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:02,580
Why were you so cruel to him?

427
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,997
I know I was wrong to talk
to Usopp without telling you.

428
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:09,817
I will admit that much, and I'm very
sorry for not being honest with you.

429
00:23:09,900 --> 00:23:13,194
But this situation did not
warrant that harsh treatment.

430
00:23:17,420 --> 00:23:18,580
May I sit down?

431
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:20,220
Go ahead.

432
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:25,397
It has been quite some time since
I first arrived at this house.

433
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:29,517
Soon it will be three years that I have
been in the service of your family.

434
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:33,894
I will never forget what happened when
I showed up at your father's door.

435
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:45,840
My former employers were
brutal, pitiless men.

436
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:49,797
I had been kicked off the ship I worked
on for some small blunder I had made,

437
00:23:49,880 --> 00:23:53,960
and with no work or money, I was on
the verge of dying like a beggar.

438
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:10,877
As far as I know, I would have
died without your father's help.

439
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:12,860
I owe my life to your parents.

440
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:17,320
And now that they're gone,
I owe my life to you.

441
00:24:19,320 --> 00:24:22,797
I know that it isn't my place to
interfere with your friendships.

442
00:24:22,880 --> 00:24:27,586
I wouldn't have taken such a bold step
unless I thought it absolutely necessary.

443
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:32,018
It's just that Usopp isn't exactly
known for his upstanding character.

444
00:24:33,060 --> 00:24:33,890
You see,

445
00:24:34,020 --> 00:24:37,597
if anything were to happen to you it
would be my fault for not seeing to

446
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:41,697
your safety and I would never be able
to ask forgiveness from your parents.

447
00:24:41,780 --> 00:24:45,486
Did you think ill of me for my
childish behavior earlier today?

448
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:48,180
No, of course I don't.

449
00:24:48,480 --> 00:24:51,245
I'm thankful to have you
around too, Klahadore.

450
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:54,237
But I stand by my opinion.

451
00:24:54,320 --> 00:24:56,377
Usopp is one of the nicest people I know.

452
00:24:56,460 --> 00:24:57,780
We'll see about that.

453
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:00,137
Until then, he is still not welcome here.

454
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:01,050
Clawhead door!

455
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:02,880
You blockhead!

456
00:25:03,420 --> 00:25:05,067
Blockhead will do just fine.

457
00:25:09,300 --> 00:25:10,460
Where'd Luffy go?

458
00:25:11,160 --> 00:25:12,160
Who knows?

459
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:14,459
He probably ran after that captain.

460
00:25:14,900 --> 00:25:16,780
I bet he's at his secret spot.

461
00:25:16,940 --> 00:25:18,440
Yeah, over by the shore.

462
00:25:18,680 --> 00:25:21,377
He always goes there when he's
going to think something over.

463
00:25:21,460 --> 00:25:24,597
Luffy can take care of himself,
but where's your other friend?

464
00:25:24,680 --> 00:25:25,820
Oh, yeah, Onion.

465
00:25:26,460 --> 00:25:29,297
Come to think of it, he just
up and disappears a lot.

466
00:25:29,380 --> 00:25:32,851
Yeah, and then he shows up out
of nowhere, all freaked out.

467
00:25:34,400 --> 00:25:35,680
Terrible news, guys!

468
00:25:36,280 --> 00:25:37,110
You see?

469
00:25:37,740 --> 00:25:38,780
Listen to me!

470
00:25:40,020 --> 00:25:43,297
Some nut job is on his way here
and he only walks backwards!

471
00:25:43,380 --> 00:25:44,210
You liar!

472
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:45,460
It's the truth!

473
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:48,380
There he is! Hmm?

474
00:25:56,520 --> 00:25:59,991
Now which one of you people
has the nerve to call me weird?

475
00:26:02,840 --> 00:26:04,380
I'm just a normal guy.

476
00:26:05,660 --> 00:26:06,490
That's so.

477
00:26:06,620 --> 00:26:08,560
You look insane. Don't be silly.

478
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:11,557
I'm just an ordinary
run-of-the-mill hypnotist.

479
00:26:11,640 --> 00:26:14,100
A hypnotist? Really? Awesome!

480
00:26:14,460 --> 00:26:15,519
Show us something!

481
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:17,080
Yeah, do something!

482
00:26:18,360 --> 00:26:19,190
What?

483
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:21,157
You fools!

484
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:23,937
What makes you think that I
would want to reveal my masterful

485
00:26:24,020 --> 00:26:27,138
hypnosis techniques to a
bunch of roadside strangers?

486
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:28,640
Okay, you convinced me.

487
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:30,257
Now watch the ring closely.

488
00:26:30,340 --> 00:26:31,170
What a ham.

489
00:26:31,360 --> 00:26:34,536
So, by the count of Django,
you'll be in a deep sleep.

490
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:36,131
All right, are you ready?

491
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:39,780
One, two, Django!

492
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:45,440
Who is this freaky weirdo?

493
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:51,290
Hi!

494
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:53,692
What are you doing here?

495
00:26:53,840 --> 00:26:56,837
I came here to be alone, but
I guess that's not happening.

496
00:26:56,920 --> 00:26:58,100
Did you follow me?

497
00:26:58,260 --> 00:26:59,217
What do you want?

498
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:00,800
Oh, you know, nothing.

499
00:27:04,420 --> 00:27:05,250
Ouch.

500
00:27:12,180 --> 00:27:14,100
I can't believe that butler.

501
00:27:14,540 --> 00:27:16,364
Insulting my old man like that.

502
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:18,437
Yeah, I hate that guy too.

503
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:22,060
No matter what anyone says,
Yasop was a great pirate.

504
00:27:22,300 --> 00:27:23,180
Yeah, really.

505
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:27,477
I've always looked up to my old man for
living every day with his life on the line,

506
00:27:27,560 --> 00:27:28,837
out in the open ocean.

507
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:31,497
He made a brave choice,
and I admire him for that.

508
00:27:31,580 --> 00:27:35,057
And then that stupid, snotty
butler stomped all over my pride.

509
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:37,400
He's gonna pay for this. Hey, wait.

510
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:40,718
Do you know if you're ever
gonna see that girl again?

511
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:42,390
I don't know.

512
00:27:43,980 --> 00:27:47,977
I'll consider it if that butler
apologizes to me for the things he said.

513
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:49,472
That butler right there?

514
00:27:50,080 --> 00:27:52,297
Yeah, that's the one. If he...

515
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:54,637
What in the world is he doing down here?

516
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:55,900
That's a big idea.

517
00:27:57,220 --> 00:28:01,660
Hey, Jango, I specifically ordered
you to keep yourself inconspicuous.

518
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,294
That doesn't mean sleeping in the road.

519
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:05,400
Relax, man.

520
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:07,797
I've perfected the
skills of the chameleon.

521
00:28:07,880 --> 00:28:09,000
I blend in.

522
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:12,017
There's someone else with
him, and man, is he weird.

523
00:28:12,100 --> 00:28:13,417
I've never seen him before.

524
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:14,330
Who is he?

525
00:28:14,413 --> 00:28:18,780
So, have all the necessary
preparations been made?

526
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:20,077
But of course.

527
00:28:20,160 --> 00:28:25,877
We can start any time!

528
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:28,780
On the rich girl murder plan?

529
00:28:32,660 --> 00:28:33,580
Murder?

530
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:48,597
searching for a great treasure.

531
00:28:48,680 --> 00:28:55,197
I had a map in my mind
that I knew would lead me.

532
00:28:55,280 --> 00:28:57,000
I had to find

533
00:28:57,840 --> 00:29:02,557
my way to that place before
someone could beat me.

534
00:29:02,640 --> 00:29:08,497
The promised land was in my
grasp And I wouldn't let go.

535
00:29:08,580 --> 00:29:13,857
My only dream.

536
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:18,977
I won't stop chasing

537
00:29:19,060 --> 00:29:21,840
it until it comes true.

538
00:29:23,300 --> 00:29:27,117
But if the world ever changes!

539
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:30,597
I call out your name.

540
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:34,077
We return to the time.

541
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:37,197
When everything's the same.

542
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:41,097
As long as I'm with you.

543
00:29:41,180 --> 00:29:43,940
My memories won't fade.

544
00:29:44,700 --> 00:29:49,240
Waiting for that one true dream?
